⭕start the server

import express from "express";
const port=process.env.port || 5001;

server.listen(port,(req,res)=>{
  console.log(`app is listening on ${port}`);
  connectDB();
})


⭕setup middlewares
1️⃣app.use(express.json());
This middleware parses incoming JSON data in the request body and makes it available as req.body.

Why needed:
Without this middleware, req.body would be undefined for JSON payloads.
It’s the replacement for body-parser.json() (which used to be a separate library).

// If client sends:
{
  "username": "Rishabh",
  "password": "12345"
}
Then in your route handler:

app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  console.log(req.body.username); // "Rishabh"
});

2️⃣app.use(cookieParser());
Purpose:
This middleware parses the cookies attached to the client request object and populates them into req.cookies.

Why needed:
Cookies are stored as plain strings in the HTTP headers — this middleware helps extract and parse them easily

If the browser sends this HTTP header:
Cookie: token=abc123; theme=dark

Then in your route:
app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  console.log(req.cookies); // { token: 'abc123', theme: 'dark' }
});

3️⃣app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));
Purpose:
This middleware parses form data (like HTML form submissions with application/x-www-form-urlencoded)

Parameter:
extended: true → allows nested objects (via qs library).
extended: false → only supports simple key-value pairs (via querystring module).

Example with extended: true:
user[name]=Rishabh&user[age]=21
becomes:
req.body = { user: { name: 'Rishabh', age: '21' } };

Example:
If a form sends:
username=Rishabh&password=12345

Then req.body becomes:
{ username: "Rishabh", password: "12345" }

4️⃣app.use("/uploads", express.static("uploads"));
Purpose:
This serves static files (like images, PDFs, etc.) from the uploads directory.

If you have a file at:
uploads/profile.jpg

You can access it via:
http://localhost:5000/uploads/profile.jpg

Why needed:
Without this middleware, the server wouldn’t expose files from the file system to HTTP response

5️⃣
app.use(cors({ origin: "http://localhost:5173", credentials: true }));
Purpose:
Enables Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) — allows your frontend (React app) hosted on 
http://localhost:5173 to make requests to your backend (e.g., http://localhost:5000).

Options explained:

origin: "http://localhost:5173"
→ Only allows requests from this frontend origin.
credentials: true
→ Allows cookies or authentication headers to be sent in cross-origin requests (important for sessions / JWT tokens in cookies).

Example:
Without this, browsers would block requests from your React frontend to your Node.js backend due to CORS restrictions.

⭕routes setup
app.use("/api/auth",authRoutes); //now in authRoute,all the routes related to authRoutes will be listed
we make separate folder of routes and contain route.js folder

app.use("/api/messages",messageRoutes);
